M="pharm-gi"
BOSS=dict(th="เคสใหญ่: ปวดลิ้นปี่ถ่ายดำ",en="Boss case: Epigastric pain and black stools",
case="A 52-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis takes naproxen daily. He has 2 months of burning epigastric pain that improves with meals, and 2 days of black tarry stools. Endoscopy: duodenal ulcer; biopsy urease test positive.",
qs=[
("Two main causes of his ulcer:",["H. pylori and NSAID use","Zollinger–Ellison and alcohol","Crohn disease and steroids","Stress and smoking only"],0,"Both are the commonest causes of PUD."),
("First-line H. pylori regimen (where clarithromycin resistance is high):",["Bismuth quadruple therapy","PPI monotherapy for 8 weeks","Clarithromycin triple therapy","Misoprostol plus sucralfate"],0,"Clarithromycin triple therapy only where resistance is low. Quadruple = PPI + bismuth + metronidazole + tetracycline."),
("PPIs act by:",["Irreversibly inhibiting H⁺/K⁺-ATPase","Blocking parietal cell H₂ receptors","Neutralizing secreted gastric acid","Coating the ulcer base in acid"],0,"Omeprazole and others are activated in the acidic canaliculus of the parietal cell."),
("Which drug prevents NSAID-induced ulcers by replacing prostaglandins?",["Misoprostol","Sucralfate","Bismuth","Famotidine"],0,"PGE₁ analog; contraindicated in pregnancy (abortifacient)."),
("Long-term PPI use is associated with:",["C. difficile infection and hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis","Gynecomastia and impotence","Hypoglycemia and weight gain"],0,"Also ↓ B12 absorption and fractures. Cimetidine (H₂ blocker) is the one that causes gynecomastia."),
("Bismuth turns stools:",["Black","Green","Red","Pale"],0,"Can mimic melena; benign."),
],pass_=4)
T={
"gimap":[("Which receptor do ondansetron and granisetron block?",["5-HT₃","D₂","NK₁","5-HT₄"],0,"Central and peripheral 5-HT₃ antagonism prevents emesis."),
 ("Sucralfate works by:",["Binding the ulcer base as a protective barrier","Blocking H₂ receptors on parietal cells","Irreversibly inhibiting the H⁺/K⁺-ATPase","Replacing mucosal prostaglandin E₁"],0,"Needs acid to polymerize; avoid with PPIs."),
 ('A 55-year-old man receiving cisplatin is given an IV antiemetic before each cycle. He later reports headache and constipation, and his QTc is 500 ms. Which receptor does this drug block?', ['5-HT₃ (serotonin)', 'D₂ (dopamine)', 'NK₁ (substance P)', 'H₁ (histamine)'], 0, 'Ondansetron (5-HT₃ antagonist) causes headache, constipation and QT prolongation. NK₁ antagonists (aprepitant) are added for delayed emesis.', 3)],
"acid":[("Cimetidine is notable for:",["CYP450 inhibition and antiandrogen effects","CYP450 induction, lowering warfarin levels","Irreversible proton pump inhibition","Hyperkalemia via aldosterone blockade"],0,"Gynecomastia, impotence; crosses BBB and placenta."),
 ("PPIs are best taken:",["30–60 min before a meal","With a bedtime snack","1–2 hours after meals","Only together with antacids"],0,"Proton pumps are activated by meals."),
 ('A 61-year-old man on stable warfarin buys an over-the-counter drug for heartburn. Four weeks later his INR has risen from 2.4 to 4.6 and he has tender breast enlargement. Which drug did he most likely start?', ['Cimetidine', 'Famotidine', 'Calcium carbonate', 'Magnesium hydroxide'], 0, 'Cimetidine inhibits CYP450 (↑ warfarin) and has antiandrogen effects causing gynecomastia. Famotidine has neither effect to a meaningful degree.', 3)],
"mucosal":[("Magnesium hydroxide antacids cause:",["Diarrhea","Constipation","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia only"],0,"Aluminum hydroxide constipates; calcium carbonate can cause hypercalcemia."),
 ("Misoprostol is contraindicated in:",["Pregnancy","Elderly","Diabetes","Hypertension"],0,"Uterine contraction; also used for medical abortion and labor induction."),
 ('A 64-year-old man with a benign gastric ulcer is prescribed a drug that forms a viscous gel adhering to the ulcer base. Which concurrently prescribed drug would most reduce its effectiveness?', ['Omeprazole', 'Amoxicillin', 'Acetaminophen', 'Metoclopramide'], 0, 'Sucralfate needs an acidic pH to polymerize, so acid suppression with a PPI reduces its effect. Amoxicillin does not alter gastric pH.', 3)],
"octreotide":[("Octreotide is a:",["Somatostatin analog","Dopamine agonist","GLP-1 agonist","5-HT₃ antagonist"],0,"Used for variceal bleeding, acromegaly, carcinoid and VIPoma."),
 ("A long-term adverse effect of octreotide:",["Cholelithiasis","Hyperkalemia","Agranulocytosis","Gynecomastia"],0,"Also nausea, cramps, steatorrhea."),
 ('A 58-year-old man has episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhea and wheezing. Echocardiography shows tricuspid regurgitation, urinary 5-HIAA is elevated and CT shows multiple liver metastases. Which drug is best for controlling his symptoms?', ['Octreotide', 'Ondansetron', 'Loperamide', 'Propranolol'], 0, 'Carcinoid syndrome is controlled with a somatostatin analog, which inhibits release of serotonin and other tumor hormones. Loperamide treats diarrhea only, not flushing.', 3)],
"ibd":[("Sulfasalazine is cleaved by colonic bacteria into:",["5-ASA and sulfapyridine","4-ASA and sulfadiazine","Mesalamine and prednisolone","6-MP and sulfapyridine"],0,"Sulfapyridine causes oligospermia and sulfa toxicity."),
 ("For moderate–severe Crohn disease, which biologic is used?",["Infliximab or adalimumab","Ondansetron or granisetron","Octreotide or lanreotide","Loperamide or diphenoxylate"],0,"Anti-TNF; screen for latent TB first."),
 ('A 29-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease is about to start infliximab. He was born in a country with high tuberculosis prevalence. Which test should be done before the first dose?', ['Interferon-γ release assay', 'TPMT enzyme activity', 'Serum anti-dsDNA titer', 'G6PD enzyme level'], 0, 'TNF-α blockade can reactivate latent TB, so screen with IGRA or tuberculin skin test. TPMT activity is checked before azathioprine/6-MP.', 3)],
"antidiar":[("Loperamide reduces diarrhea through:",["Peripheral μ-opioid agonism","Central 5-HT₃ receptor blockade","Bile acid sequestration","Muscarinic receptor blockade"],0,"Poor CNS penetration; low addictive potential; slows intestinal motility."),
 ("Antimotility agents should be avoided in:",["Bloody diarrhea from invasive infection","Traveler’s diarrhea without fever","IBS with predominant diarrhea","Chronic functional watery diarrhea"],0,"Risk of toxic megacolon or HUS. Examples: C. difficile, Shiga toxin–producing E. coli."),
 ('A 32-year-old woman with diarrhea-predominant IBS uses an over-the-counter μ-opioid agonist that slows gut transit. At usual doses she has no sedation or euphoria. Which property best explains the lack of CNS effects?', ['P-glycoprotein efflux at the BBB', 'Selective κ-opioid receptor binding', 'Absent affinity for μ receptors', 'Action at 5-HT₃ receptors in gut'], 0, 'Loperamide is pumped out of the CNS by P-glycoprotein, so effects stay peripheral. It is a μ (not κ) agonist.', 3)],
"antiemetic":[("Metoclopramide is a:",["D₂ antagonist and prokinetic","NK₁ antagonist for delayed emesis","H₁ antagonist with sedation","Cannabinoid CB₁ receptor agonist"],0,"Used for gastroparesis; causes EPS and tardive dyskinesia."),
 ("Aprepitant blocks:",["NK₁ (substance P) receptors","D₂ (dopamine) receptors","5-HT₃ (serotonin) receptors","M₁ (muscarinic) receptors"],0,"Added for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting."),
 ('A 47-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken a prokinetic drug for 5 months. She now has involuntary lip-smacking and tongue-thrusting movements. Blockade at which site explains this effect?', ['D₂ receptors in the nigrostriatal tract', '5-HT₄ receptors in myenteric plexus', 'M₁ receptors in the basal ganglia', 'H₁ receptors in the vomiting center'], 0, 'Metoclopramide blocks D₂ receptors, causing tardive dyskinesia with long use (limit to ≤ 12 weeks). 5-HT₄ agonism is a prokinetic mechanism, not a cause of dyskinesia.', 3)],
"laxative":[("Lactulose is used in hepatic encephalopathy because it:",["Is converted by gut flora to acids that trap NH₄⁺","Acts as a stimulant laxative like senna","Increases dietary protein absorption","Blocks hepatic ammonia synthesis directly"],0,"Osmotic laxative; rifaximin is often added."),
 ("Methylnaltrexone treats:",["Opioid-induced constipation","Diarrhea-predominant IBS","C. difficile colitis","Diabetic gastroparesis"],0,"Peripheral μ antagonist that does not cross the BBB."),
 ('A 56-year-old man with cirrhosis becomes confused 2 days after a variceal bleed. He has asterixis and plasma ammonia 140 µmol/L. Which mechanism underlies the first-line drug for his condition?', ['Colonic acidification trapping NH₄⁺', 'Inhibition of hepatic urea synthesis', 'Plasma binding of free ammonia', '↑ Hepatic glutamine synthetase'], 0, 'Lactulose is metabolized by colonic bacteria to acids that convert NH₃ to non-absorbable NH₄⁺ and act as an osmotic laxative. It does not act on hepatic ammonia metabolism.', 3)],
"orlistat":[("Orlistat works by:",["Inhibiting gastric and pancreatic lipase","Suppressing appetite via central 5-HT₂C","Blocking intestinal glucose absorption","Agonizing GLP-1 receptors to slow emptying"],0,"Steatorrhea; ↓ fat-soluble vitamin absorption."),
 ("A GLP-1 agonist approved for weight loss:",["Semaglutide","Metformin","Pioglitazone","Glipizide"],0,"Liraglutide and semaglutide; GI side effects."),
 ('A 38-year-old woman with obesity began a weight-loss drug 6 months ago and reports oily stools and fecal urgency after fatty meals. She now has difficulty seeing in dim light. Which supplement should have been advised?', ['Vitamins A, D, E and K', 'Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)', 'Folic acid (vitamin B9)', 'Ferrous sulfate (iron)'], 0, 'Orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase, causing steatorrhea and fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption (vitamin A → night blindness). B12 absorption is unaffected.', 3)],
"pud":[("Zollinger–Ellison syndrome is treated with:",["High-dose PPI","Misoprostol","Antacids alone","Sucralfate"],0,"Gastrinoma causes refractory ulcers; resect if possible."),
 ("First-line therapy for GERD with erosive esophagitis:",["PPI","Antacid only","Misoprostol","Metoclopramide alone"],0,"PPIs heal esophagitis better than H₂ blockers."),
 ('A 71-year-old woman with severe knee osteoarthritis must continue daily naproxen. She had a bleeding gastric ulcer last year; H. pylori testing is negative. Which strategy best reduces her risk of recurrent ulcer?', ['Add a daily proton pump inhibitor', 'Add an antacid after each meal', 'Add sucralfate before each meal', 'Add a standard-dose H₂ blocker'], 0, 'PPIs (or misoprostol) effectively prevent NSAID-induced ulcers. Standard-dose H₂ blockers prevent duodenal but not gastric NSAID ulcers.', 2)],
"cirrhosis":[("Nonselective β-blocker used to prevent variceal bleeding:",["Propranolol or nadolol (or carvedilol)","Metoprolol or bisoprolol (β₁-selective)","Esmolol (ultra-short-acting IV)","Atenolol or nebivolol"],0,"Lowers portal pressure."),
 ("Diuretic of choice for cirrhotic ascites:",["Spironolactone (± furosemide)","Hydrochlorothiazide (± amiloride)","Acetazolamide (± mannitol)","Mannitol (osmotic diuretic)"],0,"Secondary hyperaldosteronism drives sodium retention."),
 ('A 57-year-old man with cirrhosis has medium-sized esophageal varices without prior bleeding and is started on propranolol rather than metoprolol. Which effect of β₂ blockade explains this choice?', ['Unopposed α₁ splanchnic vasoconstriction', 'Direct relaxation of hepatic stellate cells', '↑ Hepatic clearance of ammonia', 'Natriuresis reducing plasma volume'], 0, 'Nonselective β-blockers lower cardiac output (β₁) and, via β₂ blockade, allow unopposed α₁ splanchnic vasoconstriction, reducing portal inflow. They do not affect ammonia clearance.', 3)],
}
